   PURPOSE AND INTENT

      Projects in use of this disclaimer in part or fully implement restrictions
      on the use of methods and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the 
      designing, planning and building software.

      The intention of this disclaimer is to clearly identify the human effort put
      into the development process, where a great amount of care was given to the
      final product without offloading the thinking purely to AI tooling. Keenly, 
      this disclaimer indicates that the learning and understanding that come as 
      byproducts of developing software were not outsourced from the engineers that 
      built the project.

   TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF USE

    FOUNDATIONAL TENANTS

      1. As the author of the software accompanying this disclaimer, you must be able
      to reason about and explain your code. It is unacceptable to use the phrase "the 
      AI wrote this, I don't know what it does".

      2. You must be able to demonstrate knowledge of the domain for which your software
      resides in.

      3. You must not impersonate yourself in the form of chatbots, agents, or other means
      as it relates to the function of this software.

      4. You are directly responsible for all of the code contributed to this project.
      

    ACCEPTABLE USE CRITERIA

      Software built with the assistance of AI in the form of complementary use is
      in full alignment to this disclaimer.

      Complementary use can be defined as, or in good faith aiming to:

        1. Increase learning potential: Whereas an engineer may use AI tools to 
        rapidly digest and understand complex topics, or quickly be introduced
        to new repositories in the sole aim of helping that engineer learn.

        2. Validate understanding: Whereas an engineer may use AI to check their
        own understanding of topics, providing quick feedback to the engineer 
        before making decisions on a topic.

        3. AI Rubber Ducking: Whereas an engineer uses a combination of the
        previous two points and AI serves as an advanced search engine to bolster
        the skillet of an engineers understanding of an area. The core principle
        of the previous 3 topics is the use of AI to improve the engineer's abilities.

        4. Mundane Boilerplate Automation: Whereas AI tooling, primarily generated
        code, can be leveraged to increase velocity of work while not taking away 
        from the engineers ability to fully understand the principles of the project
        they are building.

        5. Stubbing of Code: Whereas the engineer can use AI generated code and
        comments as a foundational piece of the project, where the goal is not to 
        lack understanding, but purely to increase velocity.

        6. Guided Implementation: Whereas the engineer can use AI tooling
        to generate code by using previously written code as a working example for
        the AI tools to use as a baseline for implementation. If the engineer has 
        already implemented a given piece of functionality, then there is a low potential
        of that not being able to be automated with AI tooling without detriment to any
        learning that may occur in the development cycle.

